00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:39.760
It doesn't have that, but it does have the same learn the pattern and hit appropriately style that Mega Man.

00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:44.900
Yeah, I think that that hits the nail on the head because there are just too many games that we've played where it's just like, all right, I'm at the boss battle.

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:48.500
He's just throwing a bunch of shit at me like I got to run and dodge, dodge, dodge and try and hit.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:51.100
And it's like, no, these all have like, yeah, something learnable.

00:22:57.460 --> 00:23:04.780
Moving into it as an adventure game, we were talking about, you know, the branching paths and the backtracking and stuff like that.

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:13.660
I wanted to offer a thought about a game we'll be playing soon, Super Mario Brothers 3 and really Super Mario World on the Super Nintendo.

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:24.580
When you beat a level in either of those games, you're not forced to replay the level in the overworld to backtrack through the world map.

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:31.340
You just now can like, you know, those levels like change color, at least in Super Mario World, they change color to mark that they've been beaten.

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:34.100
And then you can walk back to those areas.

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:39.700
Do you think that Clash of Demon Head would have benefited at all from that approach where?

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:43.820
I don't. I think that these are two very different kinds of games.

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:45.000
One is an adventure.

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:51.780
Like, imagine in a Final Fantasy game, you didn't have to fight anything once you went through a place before.

00:23:52.040 --> 00:23:54.920
Like, it's trying to sell a different experience.

00:23:55.620 --> 00:23:59.080
Mario is a platforming game and you beat the level.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:10.240
It just happens to have like a level select that makes it look like a world map, even though there really isn't any interplay between those levels.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:14.800
In like what an RPG or adventure game has.

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:18.060
So I think that that would be weird.

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:25.780
Yeah, I agree with you because I feel like, well, for one, it's hard to even like put my finger on like how to say it exactly.

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:34.140
Like Mario is trying to be much more of like this like planned out like Twitch reflex type platformer.

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:41.780
You got to jump to like every situation you find yourself in is super planned out to be like a difficult platforming challenge.

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:43.720
And I'm not saying this game isn't planned out.

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:45.720
But like you said, Sean, it's planned for like a different thing.

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:48.680
It's trying to make it seem like, no, you're this guy, a real guy.

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:52.320
I mean, a real guy is real as a guy with like jet boots or whatever it gets.

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:57.340
But like a real guy on the adventure going through like an actual like terrain and fighting things.

00:24:57.340 --> 00:25:01.300
It's not as much like platforming challenges as it is fighting enemies.

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:05.860
So it would be weird if like, I mean, I'm sure that, you know, you could just skip over it.

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:17.300
But I feel like that is for better or for worse, like this, this mountain or whatever it is path leading up to the mountain of these levels is like the meat and potatoes of the game.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:20.680
So like you beat one thing once you have to go back and suddenly you're just zipping around the map.

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:23.380
I feel like you're cutting out a lot of what the game is.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:28.220
I can see people criticizing that because you're because like your game might be artificially inflated.

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:32.920
But I think it it adds some stakes to like going back and whatnot.

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:49.160
Well, to split it the other way, then would the game have benefited at all to take the Simon's Quest, Fizanadu style approach where, you know, yeah, you're always in this platforming, side scrolling style of game.

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:51.180
But you never go back to an overworld.

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:54.840
You just go to the next area and it's all seamless.

00:25:55.240 --> 00:26:03.240
I think that might have been hard to do the I mean, I guess if you really like rethink the way that the game is designed, it might not be so hard.

00:26:03.300 --> 00:26:05.340
But it'd be tough to do the branching paths like that.

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:07.720
And like this this sort of maze of like learning.

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:09.320
I go here next. I got to go there next.

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:12.760
I don't know. It's hard for me to envision it like that.

00:26:13.060 --> 00:26:19.240
Whereas like, yeah, like Fizanadu does that does what this game does with like the backtracking going back to an extent.

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:24.080
But I feel like the whole point of this game seems to be like learn the terrain at the whole point.

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:31.280
But like a major point of this game is like learn the terrain and navigate around, find your power ups, know where things are.

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:33.640
I don't know. I don't I'm not saying I don't think it could work.

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:35.740
I think that this was like explicitly.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:37.140
It feels like their intent.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:38.500
Absolutely. Yeah.

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:44.420
It's trying to do the consequence or reward of your own choice on those branching paths.

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:47.880
There's a reason why it's not just a straight line that you move through.

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:52.500
So I'm I'm not advocating for either style Super Mario World or Fizanadu.

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:59.460
I'm I'm just arguing that those are other games that we've seen take the same kind of approach for for those for better or worse.

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:02.740
But, you know, Clash of Demon Head is doing it its own way.

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:12.200
And I think that's ultimately what we talk about here is that the choose your own path nature and whether you get lost or or find what's necessary.

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:20.620
There are areas that you don't have to go to, but they could make you stronger or collect more money to buy at the shops.

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:26.600
There are some parts of the world that are gated behind specific power ups that you might not know you need when you first get there.

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:28.000
But then you have to go find them.

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:33.940
Stage 17 has the you need that you need the jetpack to go any further.

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.900
